From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] tty: serial: qcom-geni: balance the forced runtime suspend across system sleep

0081 powers the serial engine down in system suspend with
pm_runtime_force_suspend(), except when the port stays on (wakeup source,
no_console_suspend). Two gaps:

- resume called pm_runtime_force_resume() unconditionally; it ends with
  pm_runtime_enable(), so a port that was not force-suspended got an
  unmatched enable ("Unbalanced pm_runtime_enable!").
- when pm_runtime_force_suspend() failed (e.g. the runtime suspend
  callback could not select the sleep pinctrl state), the port had
  already been shut down by uart_suspend_port(). The PM core does not run
  ->resume() for a device whose ->suspend() failed, so after the aborted
  sleep the UART stayed down; the console's ICC tag also stayed
  ACTIVE_ONLY.

Record a successful forced suspend and force-resume only then; on a
failed forced suspend bring the port back with uart_resume_port() and
restore the console's ICC tag before returning the error (also when
uart_suspend_port() fails). Found by the 2026-10-02 GPT-6.1-Sol audit.

Hardware: s2idle suspend/resume with the Bluetooth UART in use and with
the debug console, no "Unbalanced pm_runtime_enable" in dmesg; Bluetooth
works after resume.

diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
index 62db2ab49..df3951e59 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -148,6 +148,8 @@ struct qcom_geni_serial_port {
 	bool rx_tx_swap;
 	bool cts_rts_swap;
 	bool manual_flow;
+	/* ROG5: system suspend forced the SE off; resume must undo exactly that */
+	bool force_suspended;
 
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data private_data;
 	const struct qcom_geni_device_data *dev_data;
@@ -1995,7 +1997,7 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	}
 	ret = uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
 	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+		goto err_icc;
 
 	/*
 	 * uart_suspend_port() releases the port with pm_runtime_put_sync(),
@@ -2008,7 +2010,26 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	if (uport->state && uport->state->pm_state == UART_PM_STATE_ON)
 		return 0;
 
-	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
+	ret = pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * The PM core does not resume a device whose suspend failed:
+		 * bring the port back up here, or it stays shut down after the
+		 * aborted sleep.
+		 */
+		uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+		goto err_icc;
+	}
+	port->force_suspended = true;
+
+	return 0;
+
+err_icc:
+	if (uart_console(uport)) {
+		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS);
+		geni_icc_set_bw(&port->se);
+	}
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -2018,9 +2039,17 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
-	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * pm_runtime_force_resume() re-enables runtime PM unconditionally:
+	 * call it only after a forced suspend (not for a port left on as a
+	 * wakeup source).
+	 */
+	if (port->force_suspended) {
+		port->force_suspended = false;
+		ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	ret = uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
 	if (uart_console(uport)) {
